The problem asks for the measure of angle $\angle TLC$. Based on the standard naming conventions for triangles (often $ABC$ or $XYZ$) and the context of the problem, it is highly likely that $C$ refers to the vertex $Z$ of the triangle $XYZ$. If $C$ referred to the circumcenter $O$, the angle would depend on the specific configuration and is unlikely to be a constant integer. Assuming $C=Z$, we computed the coordinates of the relevant points for a specific case ($\beta=30^\circ$) and found that $\angle TLZ = 90^\circ$. This result was verified by checking the dot product of vectors $\vec{LT}$ and $\vec{LZ}$, which was zero. A second case ($\beta=60^\circ$) also yielded $\angle TLZ = 90^\circ$ after correcting arithmetic errors. This suggests that the result is independent of the shape of the triangle and is consistently $90^\circ$.

Thus, $\angle TLC = 90^\circ$.

